  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1025 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWe saw some rain showers this morning, some light snow in the \nSacramento Mountains last night and some breezy winds this\nafternoon. All of this was caused by a one-two upper level trough\npunch that moved across New Mexico in the last 24 hours. By Sunday\nmorning the upper level trough will be well to our east, this will\ngive us some light winds and our skies will see less clouds. With\nthe moisture in the ground, we could see some patchy fog develop \nSunday morning. The challenge will be how much fog will form. If \nsome clouds stick around or the winds stay up just enough we won't\nsee the fog form. Most likely locations for fog will be the \nTularosa basin, the Rio Grande Valley north of Las Cruces or the \nGila region. Aloft on Sunday we will have a dry northwest flow \nwhich will keep our high temperatures on Sunday running a little \nbelow average. \n\nOn Monday a short wave upper level ridge will move across the\nregion. Normally this will start a warming trend, but at the\nsurface we will see a weak backdoor (east to west moving) cold\nfront which will usher in some cooler air. So high temperatures on\nMonday will run 3 to 5 degrees below average. By Tuesday our upper\nlevel flow will turn more to the southwest as the next upper level\ntrough gets organized along the west coast. The southwest flow\naloft will help warm our high temperatures to near seasonal\naverages. Tuesday evening the before mentioned upper level trough\nwill move across the Four Corners area and that that will bring a\nchance for rain to the Gila region, but the rest of us will stay \ndry. We won't see much of a temperature change for our high \ntemperatures on Wednesday, but by Thursday and Friday we will see \nhigh temperatures running 3 to 5 degrees above average as yet \nanother upper level trough along the west coast strengthens our \nsouthwest flow aloft. By Friday afternoon the upper level trough \nwill be passing just north of the Four Corners area. This will be\ntoo far north to give us much of a precipitation threat, but it \nwill give us some breezy winds Friday afternoon. For next weekend,\nwe will be dry but another weak back door cold front will drop \nour temperatures back to near average or a little below average.",
